  "body": "Specification:\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/forms.html#dom-textarea/input-select\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/forms.html#set-the-selection-range\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/forms.html#dom-textarea/input-setrangetext\r\n\r\nWPT tests:\r\nhttp://w3c-test.org/html/semantics/forms/textfieldselection/\r\n\r\n```selct()```, ```selectionStart``` setter, ```selectionEnd``` setter, ```selectionDirection``` setter, ```setSelectionRange()```, and ```setRangeText()``` on INPUT and TEXTAREA elements always queue ```select``` event if they can be applied.\r\n\r\nFirefox and Chromium have it, and Safari and Edge don't have.\r\nChromium implemented the event in 2014, and the implementation completely matched to the specification.  However it caused at least two issues.\r\n\r\n*crbug.com/449470: select events consumed infinite memory*\r\nWe didn't know the actual site URLs, but we guess affected sites had something like:\r\n\r\ninput.onselect = function() {\r\n    input.selectionStart = input.selectionEnd = input.value.length;\r\n};\r\n\r\nIf we follow the specification, this function produces two ```select``` events on every ```select``` event handler.  Firefox seems to have some exceptions not defined in the specification.\r\n\r\nWe added a workaround that we don't enqueue multiple ```select``` events for a single target.\r\n\r\n*crbug.com/637860: ikea.com issue*\r\nSimilar to the previous one, but both of Chromium and Firefox dispatch ```select``` event indefinitely. Chromium had a funny behavior because of a combination with another bug.\r\n\r\n*Possible solution*\r\n* Selection API doesn't enqueue ```select``` event,\r\n* Rename ```select``` event triggered by the selection API to another name, or\r\n* Dispatch ```select``` event only if an API *changed* the selection\r\n",
